  "text": "Solution 1 is the most likely correct one, though I could not verify it independently.\n\n- **Solution 2** gives $2^e-2$, which grows only linearly in $e$. Skew morphism counts for cyclic $p$-groups grow roughly like $n^2$, so linear growth is implausible. Its family argument is also asserted rather than proved.\n- **Solution 3** gives 10 at $e=3$. I believe $\\mathrm{Skew}(\\mathbb{Z}_8)=6$ (4 automorphisms plus 2 proper skew morphisms), so this fails the base case.\n- **Solution 1** gives 6 at $e=3$, consistent with the value I recall. Its formula is integer-valued for all $e\\ge 3$, since $7\\cdot 2^{2e-5}\\equiv 2 \\pmod 3$ when $2e-5$ is odd. The recurrence $S_e=4S_{e-1}-4$ with $S_3=6$ solves to the stated closed form. The value $\\mathrm{Skew}(\\mathbb{Z}_{16})=20$ also matches my recollection, though I am not certain of it. The lifting argument behind the recurrence is unexplained, so the derivation is not checked.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
